ays  or in the second example you might have  a mobile launch where you have  a limited beta and then over time user  acquisition opens up to add more and  more concurrent players  what we're seeing more and more of today  with today's free to play games like  apex legends that incorporate  content drops seasons and live events  as you're seeing peaks happening  throughout the game's life cycle  so in today's world it's very important  to be able to scale  to these unpredictable demand peaks that  are generated by these  new types of content releases and so  we've talked about concurrency and  scaling  now let's talk about geography so using  an example game with a million  concurrent players  those players are going to be  distributed all around the earth  using this example of a typical aaa pc  console game  we would expect about 40 percent of  those concurrent players to be located  in north america we'd see about 30  percent of those players  in europe about 10 percent in south  america  and then the remaining 20 percent of  players distributed  across the middle east and asia now  because latency  matters in gaming you're going to need  to deploy your compute resources  all around the earth to get them as  close as possible to your players  so as seb and i prepared for this demo  we thought to ourselves  would it be possible to scale up all of  the compute infrastructure  required to run a global game for 1  million concurrent players  within the space of this 25-minute demo  what do you think sam with the right  tools  anything is possible all right rob  let's dive in i've created these  clusters already and i've installed a  gonais onto each of them  right now these are essentially empty  clusters running only a few nodes each  i've got one cluster in the u.s another  in the netherlands  and a third in japan because our cloud  console aggregates information across  clusters  i can see that i don't have anything  running in these clusters yet besides  the pods needed to run agonist itself  i've coded up a game server in go which  simulates a battle royale style game  it records the events which typically  take place into bigquery  our data warehouse it also creates  player information such as match  rankings  and writes those into spanner our  horizontally scalable relational  database  to deploy this server worldwide we  define it using a nagonese game server  fleet spec  combined with a google cloud game  servers deployment fleet specification  looks just like any other kubernetes  resource agonist provides an abstraction  layer on top of kubernetes to understand  the concept of a game server  or collection of game servers called a  fleet  it does this because game servers aren't  your typical stateless workload  you don't want your game to get killed  in the middle of a match and scheduled  on another note  for example likewise kubernetes needs to  know more information about the state  that the game server is in  such as total player count to handle  functions like scaling properly  agonist takes care of things like this  for us so that we don't have to  let's take a look at our current fleets  on each of the gke clusters  as you can see we don't have any fleets  deployed  let's go ahead and create a ton of game  servers  to do this i'm going to ask gcgs to  apply a new rollout  using the configs i've specified what  it'll do  is connect to every gke cluster in each  of the realms  and create the agonised fleets based  upon our fleet spec  in short we'll go from no pods to  thousands  across multiple clusters in multiple  continents  here you can see the number of servers  that we want the number which we are  booting up  and the number which are ready the  allocated column would update once  players start connecting to them  rob you asked about scaling up enough  resources for a  million concurrent players deploying  thousands of these servers across  10 000 cpu optimized cores ought to do  the trick  let's check those gke clusters remember  we only had one node in each zone  let's see what it's up to now that the  cluster auto scaler has seen our request  spike  this is great the cluster auto scaler  has already begun adding additional  nodes to these clusters  based upon the resources that we've  requested  okay more and more game servers are  coming up agones and gke are working  together to spin up additional virtual  machines to handle this load  this will take just a couple of minutes  to scale up let's check back in on these  later  back to you rob thanks sub it'll be  interesting to watch that scale up  and while that's happening let's talk a  little bit about the components that are  required to power  a global game so first you need a global  network to connect with your players  next you need high performance compute  to run your simulations and your  multiplayer matches  then you need a global database instance  to track player progress  throughout the game you need to monetize  your game so you'll need  planet scale analytics that can absorb  all the events you're going to be  posting  and then finally you need a fleet  orchestrator to bring it all together  and to scale this infrastructure up as  players join your game  and to scale it down as players move on  to other activities  let's take a look at each one of these  components in more detail  let's start with our global network the  first thing that you'll notice  on this diagram is the 24 regions  distributed around the globe  these are represented as colored circles  and are the geographies where you can  run your compute resources to power your  matches  the next thing you'll notice is our 144  edge points of presence that are located  as close as possible to your players  now the unique and differentiated thing  on this diagram  is the fiber optic network that ties our  edge points of presence  and all of our regions together only  google cloud  offers a global private fiber optic  network  out of the box next let's talk about  compute  which you'll need to power your  multiplayer matches  so we know that there are many different  types of games from a casual mobile  title that you play on your phone with  friends  to a powerful and high fidelity  multiplayer battle royale match that you  play on your pc  or console at google cloud we've created  a spectrum of compute  that you can tailor to the specific  needs of your game  so for that casual title you can run on  our cost  optimized vms and for that high fidelity  battle royale title  you can run it on our compute optimized  vms which run at high clock speed and we  built specifically  to run demanding workloads like games  next let's talk about a scalable  database as players progress through  your game  you'll need a way to capture updates  like rankings and updates to inventory  the way that a database for a global  game is typically built  is by utilizing a commodity database  like mysql  and running tens or even hundreds of  instances  to power a global game this technique is  not globally scalable  and from an ops perspective is not  awesome at google  we offer an alternative approach with  spanner  a horizontally scalable relational  database  we offer the capability to run a single  instance  that can serve all of your players  globally without shards  now that's awesome so we've talked about  a lot of different infrastructure  components that'll be required to power  your global game  it's going to be important that you can  effectively monetize  particularly in today's free-to-play  world  bigquery offers a globally scalable data  warehouse  that can scale from launch to the  world's biggest games  with zero ops for your team candy crush  is probably the best example of scaling  running on bigquery  candy crush ingests over 70 billion  events per day  seamlessly and google manages all the  ops  so to tie it all together you'll need to  scale these resources  up and down throughout the day as  players join the game  and move on to other activities to  manage the scaling of all of these  resources deployed globally  we built google cloud game servers a  global control plane for running your  game  google cloud game servers is built on  top of open source  technology including agonise and  kubernetes  which allows you to run your  infrastructure components  wherever you need them in gcp on other  clouds or on bare metal  let's take a closer look  in this diagram we're showing the game  servers api  which is your global api and control  plane for managing your game  on the left we have one kubernetes  cluster that's running in google cloud  platform  on gke on the right hand of the diagram  we have another kubernetes cluster  that's running on premise  on bare metal both clusters are being  centrally managed by google cloud  game servers and all of the operational  events and log events are being captured  centrally agonase and gcgs  offer a new cloud-native way to build  your game  let's talk about the road map for  building your game with this new  technology  first you containerize your game and  implement the open source agonise sdk  next you register each of your clusters  with the game services  api control plane then you begin  organizing your clusters into groups by  geography that we call realms  next you define configs for things like  auto scaling policies  and finally you test and roll out your  game server binary  to your global fleet of game servers  running on kubernetes  so let's check in on our scaling seb how  do you think things are looking  pretty good from here let's take a look  okay rob let's take a look and see how  our servers are coming along  so far we've got 4 200 servers running  in the u.s  a little over 3 300 running in the eu  and 500 running in japan  this is truly a global fleet more than  enough to support  1 million concurrent players as each  server can handle at least 150  players it took under 8 minutes to scale  up all the compute needed for a massive  deployment  all around the world this really  demonstrates just how easy  and powerful managed to gones really is  now let's take a look at the database  here we can see the number of nodes  we're currently using  along with the cpu utilization and  number of concurrent requests  wow that's a lot of requests and this  number should keep rising as more and  more game servers come online  we're running about 80 000 queries per  second  which is about what you would see for a  very large global game  should we need to handle more rather  than deal with database sharding  we simply increase the node count in  spanner and it handles the rest  you gotta love the separation of compute  and storage  now let's check bigquery our gaming data  warehouse where we've been ingesting  massive quantities of telemetry events  since we started our fleet  so far this table is just about a  hundred gigabytes with over 800 million  rows  excellent but let's figure out the  number of events processed per second  even though there are thousands of game  servers inserting data right now  let's ask bigquery to take the number of  events between the last two minutes and  one minute  and then divide by 60.  what i love about this is we can keep  inserting more and more data and let our  scalable databases handle the rest  when your cloud can scale to ingest data  like this your team can focus more on  creating really great games  and less on managing your infrastructure  wow over 800 000 rows per second  not bad  so rob in less than 10 minutes we scaled  up close to 10  000 cores of compute on three continents  wrote over 80 000 inserts per second to  a global database instance  and ingested over 800 million rows to  our data warehouse  talk about scalability wow  those are some impressive numbers sub we  were able to scale  all of the infrastructure required for a  million concurrent players  in about 10 minutes to recap  let's take a look at each component in  this toolbox  for building massive global games on  google cloud  our premium network offers a global  fiber optic network  extends your reach to wherever your  players are around the globe  compute optimized vms offer the high  clock speeds required for today's most  demanding games  spanner provides a global database  instance for your game with no  sharding bigquery  is a planet scale data warehouse that  can ingest  millions or billions of events each day  for your game  and finally google cloud game servers  the fleet orchestrator that can scale  these resources  up and down seamlessly during the day as  your players need them  to learn more join us on the web and  we'd like to thank you  for joining us today so seb that was a  pretty cool demo  we were able to scale up a million  concurrent player fleet  in about 10 minutes so for our viewers  what would it take to scale up a fleet  for 10 million concurrent players  well because the infrastructure and  technology are sort of taken care of for  you  the only thing that i could think of  would be more quota  you

